{
  "teamId": 115,
  "year": 2019,
  "narrative": "The 2019 Colorado Rockies were a team that never found its footing, sliding from cautious optimism in the spring to outright collapse by midsummer and finishing well short of October. Coming off back-to-back playoff appearances, the Rockies carried genuine expectations into the season, but a brutal April (11-15) set an early tone of frustration. The pitching staff — which would finish with a ghastly 5.56 ERA — was porous from the start, and while the offense could occasionally erupt behind Charlie Blackmon, Nolan Arenado, and Trevor Story, it was never enough to paper over the defensive deficiencies on the mound.\n\nMay offered a genuine reprieve, the team's best month at 16-10, fueled by Blackmon's timely home runs, Raimel Tapia's clutch extra-base hitting, and Daniel Murphy settling in as a reliable bat. For a few weeks the Rockies looked like a team that might yet make a run, and June started well enough at 15-13. But the bullpen was already showing cracks — Wade Davis, Scott Oberg, Jairo Díaz, and Jesus Tinoco were all victimized by crushing home runs during this stretch — and the Dodgers in particular feasted on Colorado's relievers in a brutal late-June series that portended darker days ahead.\n\nJuly was catastrophic. A 6-19 record was the season's death blow, and no amount of Ian Desmond home runs could stem the tide. August continued the misery at 9-19, though Nolan Arenado provided flashes of brilliance — a pair of game-changing home runs on August 14th and 18th reminded fans what the third baseman was capable of even on a sinking ship. The bullpen kept bleeding runs, the rotation kept giving them up, and the Rockies' postseason hopes were mathematically extinguished long before Labor Day.\n\nSeptember brought a measure of dignity, a 12-13 record that at least stopped the hemorrhaging. Young players like Sam Hilliard stepped into the spotlight, offering glimpses of the future with walk-off-worthy power. Charlie Blackmon hit his 31st home run in a late three-run shot, and Trevor Story capped his 35-home-run season in style. But these were consolation prizes. The 2019 Rockies — 71-91, nearly 100 runs in the red — were a cautionary tale about how quickly a contending roster can unravel when starting pitching and bullpen reliability collapse simultaneously.",
